<%# One photograph in a service page, rendered the same way everywhere.

    Takes an `img` object from content/service-pages.js:
      { base, width, height, alt, eager }

    `base` is the filename without extension in public/img. A .webp and a .jpg
    of the same name are both expected; the webp is offered first and the jpg
    is the fallback, which is what the rest of the site already does.

    Deliberately NOT here: a caption, a badge, a label, a logo overlay. The
    page around the photograph already says what the service is, and a photo
    that has to be captioned to make sense is the wrong photo.

    Width and height are always written out so the space is reserved before the
    bytes arrive -- an image that pushes the copy down as it loads is worse
    than no image. Everything is lazy except an image explicitly marked eager,
    which is for the one that may be the largest contentful paint. %>
<figure class="service-figure">
  <picture>
    <source srcset="/img/<%= img.base %>.webp?v=<%= imgVersion('img/' + img.base + '.webp') %>" type="image/webp">
    <img src="/img/<%= img.base %>.jpg?v=<%= imgVersion('img/' + img.base + '.webp') %>"
         width="<%= img.width %>" height="<%= img.height %>"
         <% if (img.eager) { %>fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"<% } else { %>loading="lazy" decoding="async"<% } %>
         alt="<%= img.alt %>">
  </picture>
</figure>
